back to the house right now i'll be back  behind my desk this afternoon  making sure that the decisions about our  next steps out of the covered lockdown  are the right ones  i said in the campaign that is my  priority for as long as it takes to get  us through crisis  and into recovery we published during  the campaign a very ambitious 100 days  programme and we'll start to implement  that immediately  of course once the crisis is over i also  said in this election i wanted to give  people in scotland the choice of  independence and i intend to do that  when we are out  of the crisis so when do you start  pushing for that referendum  well when we judge the time is right  that will not just be a judgment for me  the scottish parliament which of course  now has a majority a bigger majority of  msps in favor of an independent  referendum then was the case  before the election so the parliament  will judge when the time is right i  indicated in our manifesto that if the  covert conditions allow i would like  that to be in the first half of  this parliamentary term but covert comes  first the country  getting through and out of this crisis  comes first when i said that in the  campaign  i meant it and that's why i'm going  straight back to work to continue that  job of getting us through  this situation and in terms of  introducing the legislation  in parliament when could we expect to  see that well again that will  be uh based on the judgment as to when  the time is right we've got  a difficult few months still ahead of us  we're in a good position with covert i  hope everything is going firmly in the  right direction but we've got to make  sure that that  continues to be the case and that we get  on with the job of  delivering that 100 days plan getting a  recovery from covid  underway the legislation if a referendum  is to be delivered within the first half  of this parliament which is my  preference  covert permitting we have to introduce  the legislation in a  time frame to facilitate that happening  but of course these will be decisions  for the whole parliament  once it is back in operation next week  but that time frame suggests it would be  quite soon towards the end of this year  i'm not going to give you a date right  now because that would  not be consistent with what i've said  all the way through this campaign is  that covert comes first and i can't tell  you with certainty right now  when i in the scottish parliament will  judge that we are sufficiently out of  the crisis  to do that but that time frame is the  broad one we put in our manifesto  and obviously legislation would have to  be introduced in a time scale to  facilitate that  do you take reassurance from the words  from the uk government this morning that  they wouldn't be taking the scottish  government to court if it reached that  stage well look if we end up  in court which is not something i want  to see  that would only be because we had a uk  government that refused to accept  scottish democracy  and i think that would be an absurd  outrageous and completely unacceptable  position for them to  be in i support independence the uk  government  oppose independence that's legitimate  but the only people who have the right  to decide that question  are the people of scotland and the way  to do that is in  a referendum so you know it's this  discussion that often dominates  the debate about independence will the  uk government permit a referendum  you know it's predicated on a lack of  basic respect for scottish democracy the  snp  has just won a landslide election  victory on the strength of  a commitment to referendum when the time  is right there is a pro-independence  majority  in the scottish parliament you know the  statistics of this election campaign  in many respects are record-breaking for  the snp so  the question for the uk government is a  simple one and it should not be a  controversial one  do they accept democracy or don't they  and i hope that  however they might couch that at this  stage  they will accept democracy because  anything else is just completely  unacceptable the message from them seems  to be  not never but not now so are you in some  ways  in agreement with them over there you'd  have to ask them that i hope so i am  saying not now because as i've just said  i'm getting back to work to  continue the work to get us through  covet but when we're out of the crisis  then  people in scotland have voted for a  parliament  that says we should have an independence  referendum after the crisis  in order to shape our long-term recovery  and if the uk government has any respect  for scottish democracy which they  haven't  displayed very firmly over the past few  years but if they are prepared now to  accept scottish democracy  it should just be a question of when the  parliament of scotland elected  democratically on thursday  decides that the time is right then  justice happened in 2014 there should be  an agreement between the uk  and the scottish governments i hope that  is what will happen and  that is the situation that would be  consistent with  respect for a democratic outcome the  unionist parties are pointing to the  vote  share being split pretty much down the  middle what are you making of that  argument do you still have a case to  make to a lot of people  look having a referendum does not  automatically mean that scotland becomes  independent scotland will only become  independent if i can  persuade if the snp and the yes movement  can persuade a majority that again is  also a fundamental  principle of democracy but this argument  i mean you've got all these  desperate attempts to rewrite the basic  rules of democracy  you know on the strength of the argument  that the uk government appears to be  rather  desperately making boris johnson  wouldn't have a mandate to do anything  and you know that is the the simple  issue at stake here the s p won the  election we've won the election  with a a set of record-breaking  achievements you know we've got  more votes in the constituency ballot in  this election than any party in the  history  of devolution you'd have to go back i  think to the 1960s to find a uk party  that got  a higher percentage or as higher  percentage of the vote in a uk  general election the mandate is there  the majority in the parliament is there  that doesn't mean scotland automatically  becomes independent but it does mean  that it is for the scottish people  to decide and it strikes me that this  argument scotland's divided and split on  the question of independence  it's not an argument against the  referendum it's an argument for allowing  that to be determined  democratically because otherwise it's  politicians who make that decision and  that is not right  other than the letter from the prime  minister have you spoken to him at all  again  i'm going to speak to him later this  afternoon um you know he wrote to me  yesterday  suggesting a covert recovery summit of  course we'll be delighted to take part  in that and work constructively with  anybody to get us through clovid  and into recovery but longer term i'm  not sure that the kind of recovery that  boris johnson will envisage  is the one that a majority of people in  scotland will support and that brings to  the four this question who is it that  should shape  our long-term recovery and the kind of  country scotland becomes out of that  recovery should it be boris johnson or  should it be the people of scotland
 

